(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-anatomy-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 4 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"When the forearm is pronated, what is the position of the radius relative to the ulna?","choices":["The radius lies parallel to the ulna","The radius crosses over the ulna","The radius separates far from the ulna","The radius rotates the palm to face forward"],"answer":1,"exp":"In pronation the distal radius swings medially and crosses over the ulna, turning the palm posteriorly. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Is the tibia medial or lateral to the fibula?","choices":["Lateral","Posterior","Anterior","Medial"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tibia is medial to the fibula in the leg. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What connects the greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly on the femur?","choices":["Linea aspera","Popliteal surface","Intertrochanteric crest","Intertrochanteric line"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intertrochanteric line connects the greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How many phalanges are in each foot, and how many does the hallux have?","choices":["10 total; the hallux has 2","14 total; the hallux has 1","14 total; the hallux has 3, same as the other toes","14 total; the hallux has 2 (proximal and distal)"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are 14 phalanges in each foot, and the hallux has only 2 (proximal and distal), mirroring the thumb's pattern in the hand. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the general shape of the clavicle when viewed from above?","choices":["A single smooth C-shaped arch","A perfectly straight horizontal rod","A sharp right-angled elbow bend","A double curve, like a flattened letter S"],"answer":3,"exp":"The clavicle has a double curvature, convex anteriorly on its medial portion and concave anteriorly on its lateral portion, giving an S-like shape. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which two tubercles are found on the proximal humerus, separated by the intertubercular sulcus?","choices":["Capitulum and trochlea","Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter","Medial epicondyle and lateral epicondyle","Greater tubercle and lesser tubercle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The greater tubercle and lesser tubercle are the two proximal humeral prominences separated by the intertubercular sulcus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What forms the trochlear notch of the ulna?","choices":["The ulnar styloid process alone, at the distal end","The ulnar tuberosity alone, distal to the notch","The radial notch alone, articulating with the radial head","The olecranon and coronoid process together"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trochlear notch is formed by the olecranon (posteriorly/superiorly) and the coronoid process (anteriorly/inferiorly) together, cradling the humeral trochlea. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The surgical neck of the humerus is clinically important mainly because it is a common site of what?","choices":["Origin of the biceps tendon","Articulation with the glenoid","Attachment of a forearm membrane","A common fracture site"],"answer":3,"exp":"The surgical neck, just distal to the tubercles, is a frequent fracture site of the proximal humerus, which is why it is named 'surgical.' — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which structure connects the greater and lesser trochanters on the posterior femur?","choices":["The intertrochanteric line","The linea aspera","The gluteal tuberosity","The intertrochanteric crest"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intertrochanteric crest is the ridge that joins the greater and lesser trochanters on the posterior surface of the proximal femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"On the posterior surface of the scapula, what feature separates the supraspinous fossa from the infraspinous fossa?","choices":["The glenoid cavity","The coracoid process","The medial border","The scapular spine"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scapular spine is the ridge that separates the supraspinous fossa (above it) from the infraspinous fossa (below it) on the posterior surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The supraspinous and infraspinous fossae of the scapula are separated by which structure?","choices":["The coracoid process","The medial border","The glenoid cavity","The spine of the scapula"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scapular spine is the prominent posterior ridge that divides the posterior surface into the supraspinous fossa above and the infraspinous fossa below. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which proximal tibial ridge, seen between the condyles, is called the intercondylar eminence?","choices":["The upward bony spine between the tibial condyles","The distal projection at the inner ankle","The anterior ridge for the patellar ligament","The lateral prominence at the outer ankle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The intercondylar eminence is the raised bony ridge (spine) between the medial and lateral condyles on the superior tibial surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which four carpal bones make up the distal row?","choices":["Lunate, triquetrum, trapezoid, hamate","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate","Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","Scaphoid, trapezium, capitate, pisiform"],"answer":1,"exp":"The distal carpal row consists of the trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"The femur articulates with the tibia at the knee through which paired distal structures?","choices":["The greater and lesser trochanters","The medial and lateral malleoli","The medial and lateral condyles","The head and neck of the femur"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial and lateral condyles of the distal femur articulate with the corresponding condyles of the tibia at the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which angle of the scapula is located where the medial and lateral borders converge inferiorly?","choices":["Acromial angle","Inferior angle","Lateral angle","Superior angle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior angle is where the medial and lateral borders of the scapula converge at the bottom of the bone. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two spinal curves are the secondary curves that develop after birth?","choices":["The thoracic and sacral curves","The cervical and lumbar curves","The thoracic and lumbar curves","The cervical and sacral curves"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cervical and lumbar curves are the secondary curves, developing after birth as an infant holds up the head and begins to walk. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Between adjacent vertebral bodies, what structure acts as a cushioning pad?","choices":["The costal cartilage","The nuchal ligament","The intervertebral disc","The articular capsule of the hip"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intervertebral disc is the fibrocartilage pad between adjacent vertebral bodies that cushions and allows slight movement. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How many lumbar vertebrae are there, and what is a distinguishing feature of their spinous processes?","choices":["12; the spinous process points inferiorly","5; the spinous process points posteriorly","7; the spinous process points laterally","5; the spinous process is bifid"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 5 lumbar vertebrae, and their spinous process points posteriorly (rather than inferiorly as in most thoracic vertebrae, or being bifid as in most cervical vertebrae). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is spina bifida, per the clinical note in the material?","choices":["A malformation where the vertebral arch fails to close completely","Fusion of two or more adjacent vertebral bodies together","A traumatic fracture involving the vertebral body itself","Traumatic dislocation of the atlas from the occipital condyles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Spina bifida is a malformation of the structures making up the vertebral arch, where the vertebral arch fails to close completely. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What structure is unique to the axis (C2), and what does it allow?","choices":["The transverse foramen, allowing passage of the vertebral artery on both sides","The dens, bound to the atlas by the transverse ligament, allowing the head to move in a \"no\" manner","The bifid spinous process, permitting forward flexion of the cervical spine","The costal facet, allowing direct articulation with the first pair of ribs"],"answer":1,"exp":"The axis (C2) has a dens, which is bound to the atlas by the transverse ligament, allowing the head to pivot in a \"no\" motion. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How do the costal facets of T1–T8 differ from those of T9–T12?","choices":["There is no meaningful difference between the two groups of thoracic vertebrae","T1–T8 have no costal facets at all, while T9–T12 have two facets each","T1–T8 have superior and inferior costal facets, while T9–T12 have only one facet","T1–T8 have transverse foramina, while T9–T12 have costal facets instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"T1 to T8 have both superior and inferior costal facets, while T9 to T12 have only one facet each. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which paired facial bones form the prominences of the cheeks?","choices":["The zygomatic bones","The nasal bones","The lacrimal bones","The palatine bones"],"answer":0,"exp":"The zygomatic bones form the prominences of the cheeks and part of the lateral orbital walls. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which single bone of the orbit also contains the sella turcica and forms much of the cranial base?","choices":["The lacrimal bone","The sphenoid","The nasal bone","The vomer"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sphenoid contributes to the posterior orbit and, at the cranial base, contains the sella turcica for the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is a distinguishing feature of lumbar vertebral bodies compared to other regions, consistent with their function?","choices":["They are quite large, supporting the weight of the torso","They contain transverse foramina for the vertebral arteries","They are absent, replaced entirely by the vertebral arch","They are the smallest of all vertebral bodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lumbar vertebral bodies are quite large, reflecting their function of supporting the weight of the torso. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which ribs are the floating ribs, lacking any anterior attachment?","choices":["Ribs 1 and 2","Ribs 8 through 10","Ribs 11 and 12","Ribs 6 and 7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs; their anterior ends are free, with no attachment to the sternum or costal cartilage above. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which bone forms the roof of the orbit?","choices":["Maxilla","Zygomatic bone","Frontal bone","Sphenoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The frontal bone forms the roof of the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How many vertebrae are typically present in the adult vertebral column?","choices":["33 vertebrae","26 vertebrae","24 vertebrae","30 vertebrae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The adult vertebral column typically has 26 vertebrae, because the sacral and coccygeal vertebrae have fused into the sacrum and coccyx. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the auricular surface of the sacrum, and what does it articulate with?","choices":["A surface that forms part of the sacro-iliac joint, articulating with the ilium","The opening through which nerve roots first enter the sacral canal","A landmark serving as the attachment point for the ligamentum nuchae","A smooth surface for articulation between the sacrum and the coccyx"],"answer":0,"exp":"The auricular surface is a structure of the sacrum that forms the sacro-iliac joint by articulating with the ilium of the hip bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which smooth elevation of the frontal bone lies between the eyebrows above the nose?","choices":["The mastoid process","The glabella prominence","The mental protuberance","The occipital crest"],"answer":1,"exp":"The glabella is the smooth prominence of the frontal bone in the midline between the superciliary arches, just above the nose. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How many vertebrae fuse to form the sacrum?","choices":["3 fused vertebrae","5 fused vertebrae","7 fused vertebrae","12 fused vertebrae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sacrum is formed by the fusion of 5 sacral vertebrae into a single triangular bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which two muscles produce eversion of the foot?","choices":["Flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Fibularis brevis and fibularis longus","Tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fibularis brevis and fibularis longus both produce eversion of the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which muscle produces abduction of the thumb and is noted with an asterisk as a key muscle in the material?","choices":["Abductor pollicis longus","Extensor indicis","Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor digiti minimi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Abductor pollicis longus produces abduction of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which quadriceps muscle forms the teardrop-shaped bulge on the medial side just above the knee?","choices":["The vastus medialis","The vastus lateralis","The rectus femoris","The biceps femoris"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vastus medialis forms the teardrop-shaped muscle bulge on the medial side of the thigh just above the knee. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which intrinsic foot muscle abducts the hallux (great toe)?","choices":["Extensor digitorum brevis","Flexor hallucis brevis","Abductor hallucis","Adductor hallucis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Abductor hallucis produces abduction of the hallux. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which two muscles make up the iliopsoas group, and what is their shared action?","choices":["Iliacus and psoas major; both flex at the hip","Psoas major and gracilis; both flex at the knee","Iliacus and sartorius; both flex at the hip and knee","Iliacus and pectineus; both adduct at the hip"],"answer":0,"exp":"The iliopsoas group consists of the iliacus (flexion at the hip) and psoas major, noted as a major flexor of the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"The posterior thigh muscles known as the hamstrings extend the hip and perform which action at the knee?","choices":["Extension of the knee","Abduction of the hip","Flexion of the knee","Dorsiflexion of the ankle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hamstrings extend the thigh at the hip and flex the leg at the knee. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What are the two major groups of appendicular muscles?","choices":["Intrinsic muscles confined to the hand and foot; extrinsic muscles confined to the forearm and leg","Muscles that produce flexion at each joint; muscles that produce extension at each joint","Superficial muscles lying near the skin surface; deep muscles lying close to the bone","Muscles of the pectoral girdle and upper limbs; muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs"],"answer":3,"exp":"The two major groups of appendicular muscles are the muscles of the pectoral girdle and upper limbs, and the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Most of the intrinsic muscles of the hand, including the interossei and hypothenar muscles, are supplied by which nerve?","choices":["The ulnar nerve","The radial nerve","The axillary nerve","The femoral nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ulnar nerve supplies most intrinsic hand muscles, including the interossei, hypothenar muscles, and medial lumbricals. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which muscle allows crossing of the lower leg (as in sitting cross-legged) and flexes both the knee and the hip?","choices":["Popliteus","Semimembranosus","Sartorius","Biceps femoris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sartorius allows crossing of the lower leg and flexes both the knee and hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A rotator cuff tear most directly impairs which function of the shoulder?","choices":["Flexing the fingers into a fist","Stabilizing and rotating the arm","Dorsiflexing the foot at the ankle","Extending the knee joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rotator cuff stabilizes the humeral head in the glenoid and rotates the arm, so a tear impairs shoulder stability and rotation. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What distinguishes extrinsic muscles of the foot and toes from intrinsic muscles of the toes?","choices":["Extrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes; intrinsic muscles originate on the foot itself but move the toes","Both extrinsic and intrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg and there is no meaningful distinction in their origin, insertion, or function","Extrinsic muscles originate on the foot itself but move the toes; intrinsic muscles originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes","Extrinsic muscles move only the toes and never the ankle; intrinsic muscles move only the ankle and never the toes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extrinsic muscles of the foot and toes originate on the lower leg but move the foot and toes, while intrinsic muscles of the toes originate on some aspect of the foot itself but move the toes. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which nerve supplies the gluteus medius and minimus, whose weakness causes a Trendelenburg sign?","choices":["The inferior gluteal nerve","The femoral nerve","The obturator nerve","The superior gluteal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior gluteal nerve supplies the gluteus medius and minimus and the tensor fasciae latae, hip abductors that steady the pelvis. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which intrinsic hand muscle brings the thumb into opposition, a key movement for grasping?","choices":["Flexor pollicis brevis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Opponens pollicis produces opposition of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which long, strap-like muscle crosses the thigh obliquely and is described as the longest muscle in the body?","choices":["The gracilis muscle","The sartorius","The rectus femoris","The gastrocnemius"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sartorius is the long, strap-like muscle that crosses the anterior thigh obliquely and is the longest muscle in the body. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the specific function of quadratus plantae in the foot?","choices":["Abduction of the little toe away from the midline of the foot","Flexion of toes 2 through 5, assisting the flexor digitorum longus tendons","Extension of toes 2 through 5 at the metatarsophalangeal joints","Adduction of the great toe toward the second toe, opposite abduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Quadratus plantae produces flexion of toes 2 through 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What are the origins of the diaphragm, converging on the central tendinous sheet?","choices":["Xiphoid process, ribs 7-12 and associated costal cartilages, and anterior surfaces of the lumbar spine","Ribs 1 through 6 along with the manubrium and body of the sternum anteriorly","The iliac crest of the pelvis together with the anterior surface of the sacrum","Only the xiphoid process of the sternum, with no rib or vertebral contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm originates from the xiphoid process, ribs 7-12 and their associated costal cartilages, and the anterior surfaces of the lumbar spine, converging on the central tendinous sheet. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels innervate the pelvic floor muscles, and what is recommended for evaluating dysfunction?","choices":["S2-S4; image the spine, since the individual muscles cannot be differentiated clinically","T1-T5; perform a physical exam assessing facial movement and symmetry","L1-L3; assess hip flexion strength against manual muscle resistance","C3-C5; assess phrenic nerve function via diaphragmatic excursion on imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic floor muscles are innervated by S2-S4; since dysfunction of any individual muscle cannot be differentiated clinically, imaging the spine is recommended, with referral to GI, OB/GYN, or urology as appropriate. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Besides the abdominal muscles, which deep muscle helps flex the trunk and also flexes the hip?","choices":["The erector spinae","The trapezius muscle","The masseter muscle","The psoas major"],"answer":3,"exp":"The psoas major, running from the lumbar vertebrae to the femur, flexes the trunk and the hip. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication opens the jaw, unlike the other muscles in this group which close it?","choices":["Temporalis muscle","Masseter muscle","Lateral pterygoid","Medial pterygoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lateral pterygoid opens the jaw, distinguishing it from the masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid, which act to close the jaw. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When only one sternocleidomastoid contracts, what happens to the head?","choices":["It pulls the face straight down","It rotates the eyeball in place","It turns the face toward the opposite side","It closes the jaw tightly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Contraction of one sternocleidomastoid rotates the face toward the opposite side and tilts the head toward the same side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which muscles make up the deep layer of the urogenital triangle?","choices":["Deep transverse perineal and external urethral sphincter","Levator ani and iliococcygeus of the pelvic diaphragm","Bulbospongiosus and coccygeus, spanning both triangles","Superficial transverse perineal and ischiocavernosus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deep urogenital triangle muscles are the deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"The infrahyoid muscles, which lie below the hyoid bone, are also known by what common name?","choices":["The strap muscles","The mastication muscles","The rotator cuff muscles","The pelvic floor muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The infrahyoid muscles are called the strap muscles because of their thin, ribbon-like shape below the hyoid bone. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Into what three layers can the intrinsic (deep) back muscles be subdivided?","choices":["Cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spinal regions only","Superficial intrinsic, intermediate intrinsic, and deep intrinsic","Anterior, middle, and posterior muscular layers","Flexor, extensor, and rotator functional layers"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intrinsic back muscles can be further subdivided into superficial intrinsic, intermediate intrinsic, and deep intrinsic layers, which work to flex/stabilize/extend the spine laterally and cannot be differentiated clinically. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What are the origin and insertion of the rectus abdominis?","choices":["Origin: ribs 5 through 7 only; insertion: the pubic tubercle, without reaching the xiphoid process","Origin: the iliac crest of the pelvis; insertion: the inferior borders of ribs 6 through 12 near their angles","Origin: superior surface of the pubis around the symphysis; insertion: inferior surfaces of ribs 5-7 and the xiphoid process","Origin: the xiphoid process of the sternum; insertion: directly onto the pubic symphysis itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rectus abdominis originates on the superior surface of the pubis around the symphysis and inserts on the inferior surfaces of ribs 5-7 and the xiphoid process; it depresses the ribs and flexes the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How do the superior and inferior serratus posterior muscles differ in their action on the ribs?","choices":["Superior depresses the ribs during exhalation; inferior elevates the ribs during exhalation","Neither muscle affects rib position; both instead stabilize the scapula against the rib cage","Superior elevates the ribs for ventilation; inferior depresses the ribs for ventilation","Both muscles elevate the ribs equally during every phase of breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Serratus posterior superior elevates the ribs for ventilation, while serratus posterior inferior depresses the ribs for ventilation, both innervated by thoracic nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the specific action of the transversus abdominis, distinguishing it from the oblique muscles?","choices":["Compresses the abdomen only, without the rib-depression or lateral-flexion actions of the obliques","Elevates the ribs during forced inhalation, unlike any of the abdominal wall muscles","Extends the vertebral column, an action shared with the erector spinae group","Compresses the abdomen and also depresses the ribs, acting identically to the external and internal obliques"],"answer":0,"exp":"Transversus abdominis compresses the abdomen, without the additional rib-depression and lateral-torso-flexion actions listed for the external and internal obliques. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"The diaphragm is innervated by which nerve, arising from cervical spinal levels three through five?","choices":["The vagus nerve","The sciatic nerve","The phrenic nerve","The facial nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The phrenic nerve, from cervical levels three through five, supplies the diaphragm. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes the vertebral column laterally and is innervated by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves?","choices":["Erector spinae group","Longus colli muscle","Longus capitis muscle","Quadratus lumborum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Quadratus lumborum flexes the vertebral column laterally, innervated by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which muscles make up the superficial layer of the urogenital triangle?","choices":["Deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles of the anal triangle","Superficial transverse perineal, ischiocavernosus, and bulbospongiosus","Obturator internus and the external anal sphincter"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superficial urogenital triangle muscles are the superficial transverse perineal, ischiocavernosus, and bulbospongiosus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"The muscles of the tongue are mostly innervated by which cranial nerve?","choices":["The facial nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The trigeminal nerve","The abducens nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Most tongue muscles are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve twelve); the palatoglossus is the exception, supplied by the vagus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
